  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "This paper reviews literature on the impact of mobility on human cooperation using evolutionary game theory models. It details how spatial responses to strategic information alter social well-being, analyzing specific dynamics including passive escape, self-organized migration based on active selection, trade-offs between migration cost and speed, and multilayer network routing.",
      "why": "It unifies physical movement and information processing into a single theoretical framework, explaining how individual spatial choices can lead to macro-level social cooperation and addressing emerging human-machine hybrid network structures."
